  "body": "## What changed\r\n\r\nDual contouring (Ju, Losasso, Schaefer & Warren, \"Dual Contouring of Hermite Data\", SIGGRAPH 2002) for the machined-stock mesh, on the same cell-centre samples marching tetrahedra reads.\r\n\r\n- `stock_sim::iso`: sampling split out into a shared `Grid`; new `IsoMethod { Dual (default), Marching }` and `extract_with(field, step, method)`. `extract` / `extract_at` (the STL export and the viewer's `MeshCache`) now return the dual mesh. A dual mesh with any open edge is never returned: the marching mesh of the same samples stands in, and that is logged.\r\n- `stock_sim::dual` (new): one QEF vertex per sign-changing cell, one quad per sign-changing lattice edge, wound from material to air.\r\n- `stock_sim::hermite` (new): the Hermite data. Normals come from the slope of the crossing sheet across the parallel edges beside each crossing; the sampled gradient is the fallback. A crossing next to a ridge is marked untrusted (|va|+|vb| < 0.95·h·|n_k|), and a cell holding one solves from the trusted, same-facing planes of its 3x3x3 block instead.\r\n- `stock_sim::qef` (new): a pseudo-inverse with singular values under 0.1 of the largest dropped, so an ill-conditioned direction keeps the mass point. The vertex is clamped into its own cell, a non-finite answer falls back to the mass point, and the Jacobi eigensolver has a sweep cap.\r\n- `docs/decisions/23-dual-contouring.md`: why dual is the default (an API parameter, not a user op parameter) and why the plain-gradient normals were dropped.\r\n\r\n## Readings (cell 1.0)\r\n\r\n| fixture | dual | marching | truth |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| slot floor/wall edge, worst gap to the mesh | **0.0000** cells | 0.3606 cells | 0 |\r\n| 10x8x6 box volume | **480.000** | 472.307 | 480 |\r\n| ball-nose pocket volume | 949.775 | 948.013 | 957.458 |\r\n\r\nPlain central-difference gradient normals gave a 0.35-cell corner gap, no better than marching. That is why the sheet-slope and trusted-plane estimators exist.\r\n\r\n## Pinning tests\r\n\r\n- `crates/sim/tests/stock_dual.rs`: `dual_contouring_keeps_the_slot_floor_wall_edge_sharp` (dual < 0.05 cell, marching > 0.2 cell, both closed), `dual_is_the_default_extractor`, `a_fully_cut_field_extracts_nothing_without_error`, `a_body_under_one_cell_never_panics_and_never_opens`, `a_curved_ball_pocket_stays_closed_and_never_bulges`.\r\n- Unit tests: `qef::tests::*` (corner, edge, ill-conditioned/empty/NaN, far intersection clamped), `dual::tests::*` (empty lattice, single cell, NaN and truncated lattice).\r\n\r\n## Gate\r\n\r\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-sim`: every suite green.\r\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-sim --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\r\n- `camcraft-app --test stock_export` (2/2), `camcraft-ui --lib machined` (2/2), `camcraft-ui --test gpu_render machined_mesh_paints_as_stock` (1/1): green.\r\n\r\nChangelog: The machined-stock mesh (STL export, viewer) is extracted by dual contouring by default, so a pocket's sharp floor/wall edge survives where marching tetrahedra rounded it off; marching remains as the fallback and as a named method.\r\n\r\nFixes #23\r\n\r\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\r\n",
